  "invoice": {
    "title": "Tax Invoice",
    "requiredFields": [
      "Seller name, address and GSTIN",
      "Invoice number (unique, in sequence, up to 16 characters) and date",
      "Buyer name, address and GSTIN (a registered buyer; an unregistered buyer when the invoice is above ₹50,000)",
      "HSN code of goods or SAC of services (the number of digits depends on the seller's yearly turnover)",
      "Description, quantity, unit and value of each item",
      "Taxable value after discount",
      "Rate and amount of CGST and SGST, or IGST, and cess",
      "Place of supply (state name and code) when the sale is between states",
      "Signature of the seller or the authorised person"
    ],
    "eInvoice": "E-invoicing (an IRN and a QR code from the government portal) is compulsory for businesses above the yearly turnover limit that GSTN sets and lowers from time to time. A shop below it does not need it. Check the current limit with your accountant.",
    "retentionYears": 6
  },
  "notes": [
    "Since 22 September 2025 most goods are taxed at 5% or 18%, with 40% for sin and luxury goods; the 12% and 28% rates ended and are kept only so that old stock and old bills still read correctly.",
    "Compensation cess is entered on the line (cessPercent); it is no longer charged on most goods.",
    "The region of a bill is the GST state code (for example 27 for Maharashtra). Sales between two states are charged IGST, sales inside one state are charged CGST and SGST.",
    "Prices in India are normally printed with the tax already included.",
    "The return lists (returns) follow the lists of the GST returns. The limit for a large bill between states to a buyer without a GSTIN is written as understood on the day this pack was written (over 1,00,000; before August 2024 it was 2,50,000): an adviser must confirm it and the lists before the first return."
  ]
